# From a bare Debian 13 install to a calango-nix desktop

Generated by `home/bootstrap.nix`. Every package name, group and command below
comes from `calango.bootstrap.*`, so this file cannot disagree with the flake.

Replace `<user>` with the account name and `<host>` with the machine's
hostname throughout.

## What orders this

Six constraints, and every one of them has cost somebody something:

1. **Nix before everything.** apt supplies `nix-bin`; `nix-daemon` is a root
   service.
2. **The `nix-users` group needs a new login.**
   `/nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/` is `0770 root:nix-users`. Until you log in
   again, prefix each command with `sg nix-users -c '…'`.
3. **The repositories before the corp packages.**
   @corpRepoPackagesCount@ of @corpPackagesCount@ come from a third-party
   repository.
4. **The corp packages before `calango-desktop`.** apt resolves `Depends`
   before it unpacks anything, and @corpFileOnlyCount@ of @corpPackagesCount@
   are reachable from no repository at all.
5. **`calango-desktop` before the first login.** It ships the greetd session
   entry.
6. **The first `activate` before the first login.** The session entry runs
   `$HOME/.nix-profile/bin/uwsm`.

Each stage from A onward ends with a **gate**. Run it. Do not go on until it
answers as shown. Stage 0 has no gate of its own because nothing it does can be
observed until the machine has booted — Gate A is its gate too.

---

## Stage 0 — before Debian is installed

Skip this whole stage if the machine already has Debian on it. **It is the one
stage that cannot be done afterwards**, and it exists to make Stage A
unnecessary rather than easier.

The generated `preseed.cfg` beside this file drives Debian's installer through
the three things this flake already declares: the Stage A package list, the
group memberships, and the account name. **The account it creates is
`@username@`** — generated from this flake's `home.username`, the same value
every later stage means by `<user>`. It is not a prompt the installer shows
you; `d-i passwd/username` answers it before the installer ever asks. Serve it
from the machine you are reading this on:

```sh
B=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoBootstrap')
(cd "$B" && python3 -m http.server 8000)
```

Then boot the new machine from a Debian 13 netinst image and, at the boot menu,
edit the kernel command line to add:

```
auto=true url=http://<this-machine>:8000/preseed.cfg
```

`<this-machine>` is an address the new machine can reach — check that before you
boot the installer rather than after, because a laptop on a captive-portal
network often cannot.

**`priority=critical` is deliberately absent from that line.** It would
suppress every question below `critical`, answering each from its installer
default — which directly contradicts the next paragraph's instruction to
answer four of them yourself. `auto=true` alone is enough: it still fetches
and applies this preseed, and the questions it leaves standing are exactly
the ones this runbook already tells you to answer. The one thing worth
knowing before dropping the priority is whether it was hiding something
load-bearing, and on the account questions it is not — `user-setup-udeb`
1.107 asks all four at `critical` regardless:

```
user-setup-ask:91   db_input critical passwd/user-fullname
user-setup-ask:122  db_input critical passwd/username
user-setup-ask:153  db_input critical passwd/user-password
user-setup-ask:154  db_input critical passwd/user-password-again
```

So the account still gets a real, interactively-set password whether or not
`priority=critical` is on the line. Whether disk, locale, mirror and timezone
are asked at `critical` too is unmeasured — `choose-mirror` and `netcfg` are
compiled binaries, not greppable udebs — which is exactly why the line does
not rely on it: dropping the priority makes the next paragraph true without
needing that answer.

**Answer the installer's disk, locale, mirror and timezone prompts yourself.**
The preseed deliberately drives none of them: they are decisions about a
physical disk, and a recipe generated by this flake would either not match the
encryption this project uses or would apply encryption unattended to hardware.

**What it saves you.** The whole of Stage A, including the branch on whether the
installer put your account in `sudo` — the preseed adds that group itself. An
unavailable package name fails the install loudly rather than shipping a machine
missing one, measured: `The failing step is: Select and install software`.

**What it does not save you.** `d-i pkgsel/upgrade select none` is set
deliberately, so everything the installer selected — tasksel's standard set as
well as this flake's @basePackagesCount@ packages — stays at whatever version
the netinst image shipped, rather than whatever point release exists by install
day. A
machine installed by hand may already be ahead of that. If you want parity,
run `sudo apt upgrade` once Gate A is green.

Stop the `http.server` when the install finishes. It is unauthenticated, and it
has nothing more to serve.

### Gate 0

There is none, and that is deliberate: Gate A below is the gate for this stage
too. It counts the groups and checks the daemon on the installed machine, which
is the only place either can be observed.

---

## Stage A — as root, and only if you skipped Stage 0

**If you installed with the generated preseed, this whole stage is already
done — go to Gate A and confirm it.** What follows is for a machine that was
already installed when you got it: someone else's install, rented hardware, or a
reinstall you did not drive.

Whether `sudo` even works yet depends on how this machine was installed: the
Debian installer puts the first user in the `sudo` group only when root was
given an **empty** password. A machine installed with a real root password
leaves the first user out of `sudo` -- and `sudo` is `Priority: optional`, so a
machine nobody has run Stage A on yet may not have the *binary* installed
either, regardless of what `calango.bootstrap.packages.base` declares. That
option is one of the @basePackagesCount@ packages this stage installs; it is
not yet true of a machine that has not been through this stage.
`sudo apt install ...` on such a machine fails before it does anything, with
`sudo` reported as an unknown command.

On that kind of machine, become root directly instead of through `sudo`:

```sh
su -
apt install @basePackagesOneLine@
usermod -aG @groupsComma@ <user>
usermod -aG sudo <user>
```

Where the account already has `sudo` -- root was given an empty password, or
someone already ran the block above -- run the same three commands prefixed
with `sudo` from `<user>`'s own shell instead of `su -`. Either way, `<user>`
must end Stage A holding `sudo`: Stage C runs it from `<user>`'s own shell,
not from a root login, and `usermod -aG sudo <user>` is what puts it there.
If you are unsure which case applies, `id -nG <user>` after Gate A below
settles it.

Why each package is there:

@basePackagesTable@

### Gate A

```sh
systemctl is-active nix-daemon.service                 # active
id -nG <user> | tr ' ' '\n' | grep -cx @groupsGrepArgs@   # @groupsCount@
id -nG <user> | tr ' ' '\n' | grep -cx sudo               # 1
```

Check the **service**, not the socket. `nix-daemon.socket` reads
`inactive (dead)` for ever on a working install, because the service is
`WantedBy=multi-user.target` and binds the socket itself.

The group count is answered by `id -nG <user>`, which reads the account's
groups from the group database. Your own shell does not have them until you
log in again.

`sudo` gets its own line rather than joining the count above, because it
answers a different question: `@groupsGrepArgs@` is
`calango.bootstrap.groups`, what the desktop itself needs and the activation
hook's drift check reports on; `sudo` is what Stage C needs to run at all, and
Stage 0's whole saving is that it adds this group without anyone asking. A
gate that only counted the three would pass on a machine still missing it.

---

## Stage B — as `<user>`

The clone is over **https**, and that is an ordering fact rather than a
preference. A bare machine has no ssh key and no agent, and this project's keys
live in 1Password's agent — which Stage C installs, *after* this clone has
already had to happen. There is no order in which the `git@` remote works here.
Switch the remote to ssh later if you want to push from this machine.

**This repository is not anonymously readable, so have a credential ready
before you run the clone.** Measured from a machine that had just finished
Stage 0, and again from suffer:

```sh
GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 git ls-remote @repoUrl@ HEAD
# fatal: could not read Username for 'https://github.com': terminal prompts disabled
```

That is what a private repository looks like to an unauthenticated client, and
it does not change the paragraph above — it sharpens it. A **token** can be
typed at the prompt `git` puts up, on a machine with nothing installed on it
yet; an ssh key cannot be conjured the same way, because the key this project
uses lives in an agent Stage C has not installed. So https is still the only
transport with a working order, and the credential is a personal access token
with read access, entered when `git clone` asks.

**The remote also has to be current.** A clone of a branch that predates this
document produces a tree with no `home/bootstrap.nix` in it, and therefore no
`.#calangoBootstrap` — so nothing later can render the runbook you are
reading.
Gate B is where that shows up rather than here: `nix build .#calangoBootstrap`
fails outright on such a tree, which is the failure you want, at the earliest
point it can happen.

```sh
mkdir -p ~/.config/nix
printf 'experimental-features = nix-command flakes\n' > ~/.config/nix/nix.conf
git clone @repoUrl@ ~/Projects/calango-nix
cd ~/Projects/calango-nix
```

Write this machine's hardware facts. Only the first is required:

```sh
cp hypr/hosts/suffer.lua hypr/hosts/<host>.lua      # then edit the outputs
cp foot/hosts/suffer.ini foot/hosts/<host>.ini      # optional
cp gtk/hosts/suffer.conf gtk/hosts/<host>.conf      # optional
```

`hypr/hosts/<host>.lua` is required and the build enforces it. Without it
`primary` is nil and **every workspace rule becomes a no-op** — the 1-5 and
6-10 split, the VSCode workspace, the persistent YouTube workspace. The
desktop starts and looks correct, which is why this is a build error rather
than a runtime warning.

Add the host in `flake.nix`, beside the existing entry:

```nix
      <host> = mkHome "<user>" "<host>";
```

```nix
      hostConfigs = {
        "isutton@suffer" = suffer;
        "<user>@<host>" = <host>;
      };
```

Then build both artifacts:

```sh
sg nix-users -c 'nix flake check'
sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoBootstrap'
sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoDeb'
sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths \
  .#homeConfigurations."<user>@<host>".activationPackage'
```

### Gate B

```sh
sg nix-users -c 'nix flake check' 2>&1 | grep -o 'running [0-9]* flake checks'
```

Count the checks; do not compare against a number written down anywhere. Then:

```sh
B=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoBootstrap')
find "$B" -type f | sort
./test/apt-sources.sh "$B"        # all @aptSourceCount@ source(s) verified
```

---

## Stage C — as `<user>`, elevating with `sudo`

Each stage has to stand on its own: nothing says the same shell survives from
Stage B, and a reader who opens a fresh one here has neither `$B` nor the
repository as a working directory. Re-derive both before anything else:

```sh
cd ~/Projects/calango-nix
B=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoBootstrap')
```

**Install the repositories first.** These files are scaffolding, not state.
They exist so that one `apt install` can succeed; each vendor package then
writes its own copy from its own `postinst`, and Chrome's from
`/etc/cron.daily/google-chrome`.

```sh
sudo install -Dm644 -t /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ "$B"/etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.sources
sudo apt update
```

**Then the corp packages.** @corpRepoPackagesCount@ come from those
repositories:

```sh
sudo apt install @corpRepoPackagesOneLine@
```

**Now delete the two colliding source files, before any other apt command.**
This is not tidying and it is not optional. Two of the four vendor packages
write their own source file from their `postinst`, naming a keyring under
`/usr/share/keyrings` where the file installed above carries an inline key. apt
treats one repository reached through two different `Signed-By` values as an
**error**, not as a duplicate warning, and then refuses to read the source list
at all:

```
   != /usr/share/keyrings/google-chrome.gpg
E: The list of sources could not be read.
```

Measured on a bare Debian 13.6 machine: with those files left in place, the next
two `apt install` commands each returned exit 100 and nothing further in this
stage could run.

```sh
cd /etc/apt/sources.list.d && sudo rm -f @aptTransientFiles@ && cd -
sudo apt update
```

@aptTransientTable@

**The other files stay, and deleting them would be a mistake.** Nothing else
ever provides those repositories -- neither package ships a source file, and
neither writes one -- so removing them leaves the package installed with no
candidate version at all, the same position `slack-desktop` and `fresh-editor`
are in. Measured: after all four were deleted, `apt-cache policy code` and
`apt-cache policy endpoint-verification` each reported no source. Keep:

```
@aptDurableFiles@
```


**Then the @corpFileOnlyCount@ that come from no repository at all.** Fetch
each `.deb` and install it from the file:

```sh
./bin/slack-latest                 # prints the current version and the commands
sudo apt install ./slack-desktop-<version>-amd64.deb
sudo apt install ./fresh-editor_<version>_amd64.deb
```

Where each comes from:

@corpPackagesTable@

**Then the metapackage.** It pulls the Debian-side keeps as its own `Depends`:

```sh
D=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoDeb')
sudo apt install "$D"/calango-desktop_*.deb
```

**And the greetd configuration.** This is the one file here that can leave the
machine unable to reach a desktop, so read Gate C before rebooting:

```sh
sudo install -Dm644 "$B/etc/greetd/config.toml" /etc/greetd/config.toml
sudo systemctl enable greetd.service
```

On a bare machine dpkg asks **nothing** during any of this. On suffer
`/etc/default/slack` already existed unowned, so dpkg raised a conffile prompt
whose default answer re-armed a dead repository. Slack's `.deb` ships no such
file — its cron job creates one — so installing `calango-desktop` on the first
day means there is nothing to prompt about.

### Gate C

```sh
dpkg-query -W -f='${db:Status-Abbrev} ${Package}\n' calango-desktop   # ii calango-desktop
apt-get -s autoremove | grep -c '^Remv '                              # 0
test -f /usr/share/wayland-sessions/hyprland-nix.desktop && echo present
cmp -s /etc/greetd/config.toml "$B/etc/greetd/config.toml" && echo greetd-ok
```

---

## Stage D — as `<user>`

The first switch cannot use `home-manager`, because
`programs.home-manager.enable = true` supplies that command only after a
generation exists. Do **not** use `nix run home-manager`: that reads the flake
registry, which tracks unstable rather than this flake's pinned
`release-26.05`. Use the pinned activation package:

```sh
p=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths \
  .#homeConfigurations."<user>@<host>".activationPackage')
"$p/activate"
```

Then log out and select **Hyprland (Nix)** in tuigreet.

**`home-manager` is installed but not yet on your `PATH`, and will not be until
a graphical session has set it.** This flake manages no shell initialisation, so
nothing sources `~/.nix-profile/etc/profile.d/hm-session-vars.sh` -- measured on
a bare machine, where a login shell still reported `home-manager` absent while
`~/.nix-profile/bin/home-manager --version` answered `26.05-pre`. Until the
session provides it, name the full path:

```sh
sg nix-users -c '~/.nix-profile/bin/home-manager switch --flake .#<user>@<host>'
```

### Gate D

```sh
test -x ~/.nix-profile/bin/uwsm && echo uwsm-present
loginctl show-session "$XDG_SESSION_ID" -p Type --value      # wayland
tr '\0' '\n' < /proc/$(pgrep -x .Hyprland-wrapp)/environ \
  | grep -c LIBGL_DRIVERS_PATH                               # 1
```

The `pgrep` pattern is `.Hyprland-wrapp`, truncated at fifteen characters,
because Nix wraps the binary. `pgrep -x Hyprland` matches nothing in the
working state and the broken state alike.

---

## Stage E — what is deliberately not here

- **The live state.** Fonts, syncthing, Signal, the keyring and `~/.ssh` are
  not migrated. A second machine re-pairs and re-links.
- **The corporate enrolment.** `endpoint-verification` is a managed-device
  agent.
- **A `ufw` rule.** This flake ships the profile vocabulary; `ufw allow
  calango-syncthing` is a human act, because `/etc/ufw/user.rules` is
  `0640 root:root` and nothing unprivileged can verify a rule.

### Gate E

```sh
dpkg -V calango-desktop   # no output -- every shipped file matches its checksum
```

`dpkg -V` exits 0 whether or not it finds anything, so the gate is the
OUTPUT, not the exit status: a clean install prints nothing at all, and any
line of output names a shipped file -- the greetd session entry or
`/etc/default/slack` among them -- that has since been hand-edited or has
drifted from the package. `home/bootstrap.nix`'s own activation hook runs
this same command at every switch and reports exactly this asymmetry.
